{%- comment -%}
  Product card — the option control the design puts on the card itself.

  Picking a value moves the caption, the price and what goes in the bag — and
  deliberately *not* the photograph, which is the design's own rule: "Colour is
  chosen on the card itself: the name, the price and what goes in the bag all
  follow it. The photograph does not — we shoot one metal."

  Every control is a **real link** to the piece at that value, so a pick works
  without scripting — it simply navigates — and each value's price markup is
  rendered onto the link by Liquid, so a pick costs no request and no
  arithmetic in the browser. (The design uses a `<button>`; a button without
  script does nothing, and the theme's rule is that nothing is load-bearing
  without scripting.)

  **This block is the control and nothing else.** The name of the chosen value
  is `product-card-option-name`, a block of its own, because the caption is
  something a merchant composes: a shop selling one thing wants "White Gold",
  and a shop selling another wants the name beside two facts of its own. The
  two used to be one block and the caption was assembled here, which put this
  theme's first catalogue into the theme.

  **There is no option picker here, and that is a structural finding rather
  than a simplification.** A theme block cannot read its parent's settings and
  cannot read a sibling's, so a merchant-set "which option" would be visible to
  this block alone — the price and the button resolve their variant
  independently, and would go on answering for the option they derive. The row
  would mark one value selected while the price beside it quoted another, with
  nothing on screen to say why. So all three derive the option identically
  through `snippets/card-option.liquid`.

  **Swatches paint from the option value's own Color metafield and from nowhere
  else.** `snippets/metal-swatch.liquid` is the single place the theme decides
  what a value looks like, shared with the quick view and the product page. A
  value with no swatch configured paints nothing and keeps its hairline and its
  accessible name, so it stays pickable and announced — that is the connection
  missing in admin, not a theme fault. Never hardcode a table of values here.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign product = closest.product

  capture card_option_raw
    render 'card-option', product: product
  endcapture
  assign card_option = card_option_raw | strip | split: '||'
  assign opt_index = card_option[0] | plus: 0
  assign selected_value = ''
  if card_option.size > 2
    assign selected_value = card_option[1]
  endif

  assign card_option_drop = blank
  if opt_index >= 0
    for option in product.options_with_values
      if forloop.index0 == opt_index
        assign card_option_drop = option
        break
      endif
    endfor
  endif

  comment
    The order is `snippets/metal-order.liquid`'s: the design's own table, not
    the order the option happens to carry, with anything it does not recognise
    appended rather than dropped. That last part is what makes it safe on an
    option it knows nothing about — every value is unmatched, so the merchant's
    own order survives intact.
  endcomment
  capture ordered_values_raw
    render 'metal-order', option: card_option_drop
  endcapture
  assign ordered_values = ordered_values_raw | strip | split: '||'

  assign is_pills = false
  if block.settings.control == 'pills'
    assign is_pills = true
  endif

  comment
    Round or square, and it is one declaration rather than a second dot. The
    radius is published as `--swatch-radius`, which `.swatch-dot` reads with a
    `50%` fallback — so the dot is round wherever nothing says otherwise, which
    is every other place it is drawn (the quick view, the product page).
  endcomment
  assign swatch_radius = '50%'
  if block.settings.swatch_shape == 'square'
    assign swatch_radius = 'var(--radius-base, 0)'
  endif
-%}

{%- if card_option_drop != blank -%}
  <div
    class="{% if is_pills %}card__pills{% else %}card__swatches{% endif %}"
    {% unless is_pills %}data-card-swatches style="--swatch-radius: {{ swatch_radius }};"{% endunless %}
    {{ block.shopify_attributes }}
  >
    {%- for value in ordered_values -%}
      {%- liquid
        comment
          The card offers no second control, so a pick has to choose a variant
          on the customer's behalf: the dearest *available* one carrying this
          value. A sold-out variant cannot be bought, so preferring it would
          post something that fails at checkout; where the whole value is sold
          out the dearest overall stands in and the button says Sold out.

          This is `card-option.liquid`'s rule applied per value rather than to
          the selected one, which is why it is written out here — the snippet
          answers for the card's current state, and the row has to answer for
          every state the card could be put into.
        endcomment
        assign chosen = blank
        assign dearest_any = blank
        for variant in product.variants
          if variant.options[opt_index] == value
            if dearest_any == blank or variant.price > dearest_any.price
              assign dearest_any = variant
            endif
            if variant.available
              if chosen == blank or variant.price > chosen.price
                assign chosen = variant
              endif
            endif
          endif
        endfor
        if chosen == blank
          assign chosen = dearest_any
        endif

        assign is_selected = false
        if value == selected_value
          assign is_selected = true
        endif

        comment
          `ordered_values` came back through `split`, so `value` is a plain
          string and no longer the product_option_value drop. The drop is what
          carries a merchant's native Shopify swatch, so it is looked up again
          here — without this the swatch snippet's first and most specific
          branch is silently unreachable.
        endcomment
        assign value_drop = blank
        for candidate in card_option_drop.values
          if candidate == value
            assign value_drop = candidate
            break
          endif
        endfor
      -%}

      {%- comment -%}
        The price markup a pick writes into `[data-card-price]`. It carries the
        figure and nothing else: `theme.js` replaces that element's whole
        `innerHTML`, and anything beside the price — a note, a unit — is a
        sibling block out of its reach.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- capture value_price_html -%}
        {%- if chosen.compare_at_price > chosen.price -%}
          <s>{{ chosen.compare_at_price | money }}</s>
        {%- endif -%}
        {{- chosen.price | money -}}
      {%- endcapture -%}

      <a
        class="{% if is_pills %}card__pill{% else %}card__swatch{% endif %}{% if is_selected %} is-selected{% endif %}"
        href="{{ product.url }}?variant={{ chosen.id }}"
        data-card-metal
        data-metal-value="{{ value | escape }}"
        data-metal-id="{{ chosen.id }}"
        data-metal-available="{{ chosen.available }}"
        data-metal-meta="{{ value | escape }}"
        data-metal-price="{{ value_price_html | escape }}"
        {% unless is_pills %}data-tip="{{ value | escape }}"{% endunless %}
        aria-label="{{ value | escape }}"
        aria-current="{% if is_selected %}true{% else %}false{% endif %}"
        {% if is_pills == false and forloop.index0 >= 3 %}hidden{% endif %}
      >
        {%- comment -%}
          `selected` is deliberately not passed to the dot. The wrapping link
          carries the state and `.card__swatch.is-selected .swatch-dot` draws
          the ring from it, so there is one source of truth. Marking the dot as
          well left two rings after a pick: the script moves the class on the
          link and never on the dot.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- if is_pills -%}
          {{- value -}}
        {%- else -%}
          {% render 'metal-swatch', option_value: value_drop, size: 80 %}
        {%- endif -%}
      </a>
    {%- endfor -%}

    {%- comment -%}
      The design's "+N". Before a width is known it shows three —
      `else if (!avail) n = Math.min(keys.length, 3)` — so that is what Liquid
      renders, and the script re-fits once it can measure. Pills wrap instead of
      clipping, so they need none of it.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- unless is_pills -%}
      {%- assign value_total = ordered_values.size -%}
      <span
        class="card__swatch-more"
        data-card-swatch-more
        {% if value_total <= 3 %}hidden{% endif %}
      >{% if value_total > 3 %}+{{ value_total | minus: 3 }}{% endif %}</span>
    {%- endunless -%}
  </div>
{%- endif -%}

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "t:blocks.product_card_variants.name",
  "tag": null,
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "control",
      "label": "t:blocks.product_card_variants.control.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.product_card_variants.control.info",
      "options": [
        { "value": "swatches", "label": "t:blocks.product_card_variants.control.swatches" },
        { "value": "pills", "label": "t:blocks.product_card_variants.control.pills" }
      ],
      "default": "swatches"
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "swatch_shape",
      "label": "t:blocks.product_card_variants.swatch_shape.label",
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.control == 'swatches' }}",
      "options": [
        { "value": "round", "label": "t:blocks.product_card_variants.swatch_shape.round" },
        { "value": "square", "label": "t:blocks.product_card_variants.swatch_shape.square" }
      ],
      "default": "round"
    }
  ],
  "presets": [
    {
      "name": "t:blocks.product_card_variants.name",
      "settings": {
        "control": "swatches",
        "swatch_shape": "round"
      }
    }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}
